All three functions are implemented and `python3 test_impl.py` prints `ALL PASS` (stress loop took 2.9s); I ran it after each function, and `test_impl.py` is untouched.

- **`encode`** in `rle.py`: groups consecutive characters and emits each character followed by its run length.
- **`decode`** in `rle.py`: reads each non-digit character followed by its 1+ digit count and expands it.
- **`merge`** in `intervals.py`: sorts by start and folds each interval into the previous one when it overlaps or touches. It returns new lists and does not mutate the input.

Strings that themselves contain digits can't round-trip through this format (`encode("111")` gives `"13"`, which is ambiguous to decode). That is inherent to the format the docstrings specify, and the tests don't exercise it.
